From 57249498bdc523c7aa61b89584bef0138010d13f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 23:30:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add connect-dl recording downloader; fix client shutdown
 abort

The repository could join a live meeting but had no way to get at a recorded
one. tools/connect-dl downloads a recording and rebuilds it into playable
files, with the lecturer's audio as the deliverable that must not fail.

Adobe Connect does not hand out a video file. A recording is a set of
independent Flash-era streams - shared screen, webcam, one FLV per stretch of
microphone audio - with no manifest an ordinary player understands, so the
streams have to be identified and placed on a common timeline first.

  urls/api/fetch  link parsing for every shape a Connect URL takes, the XML
                  API (including the Enhanced Security session swap after
                  login), and the poll loop for the on-demand
                  output/...zip?download=zip archive.
  flv             a direct FLV reader. Connect's own duration metadata is
                  frequently absent or wrong, so codecs and timestamp spans
                  come from the container. Tag headers only, so it stays fast
                  on gigabyte files.
  archive         classifies the streams and resolves each one's start time
                  from the recording XML, the FLV timestamps, or - last
                  resort - file order, reporting which rule applied.
  media           one ffmpeg pass per output. Audio segments are delayed onto
                  their true positions and mixed, so a break in the middle of
                  a lecture stays a silence instead of being closed up.
  player          an offline page with the shared screen, chapters and chat,
                  and the playback speed control Connect never offered.

Alignment is decided once per archive rather than per file: a single non-zero
start anywhere means Connect stamped the streams with their real meeting
positions, so a zero elsewhere genuinely means "from the beginning". Reading
that per file instead treats a zero as unknown and appends the first half of
the lecture behind the second - covered by a regression test.

Also fixes, in the existing client:

- main.cpp ran its X11 event loop on a std::thread that was neither joined
  nor detached, so the destructor called std::terminate and a clean quit
  aborted. That thread also drove Xlib from a second thread without
  XInitThreads and tore the display down underneath the running CEF loop.
  Polling X from the single loop removes all three problems.
- the loop slept for ((1000 / 30) / 1000) seconds - integer division, so
  sleep(0) - and spun a core at 100% for the whole meeting.
- MyApp.h and MyClient.h had no include guards, and MyClient.h defined its
  constructor out of line, so a second includer would not link.
- MyClient inherited CefLifeSpanHandler but never returned it from
  GetLifeSpanHandler, leaving the base inert.
- the cache path came from getlogin(), which is the wrong user under sudo and
  fails with no tty; $HOME is used first now.
- install.sh rewrote open.sh and connect.desktop in the source tree with sed
  -i, consuming the INSTALL_PATH placeholder so a second run silently kept
  the first run's path. The files are generated instead.

README documents why CEF stays pinned at 86: Chromium dropped the PPAPI Flash
interface in 88 and the classic meeting room is a Flash application, so
upgrading would build and then fail to load any meeting. connect-dl uses
neither CEF nor Flash, so recordings are unaffected.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WbvpE7m9W5mWiGPxqtHFUL
